In this example, we first import the `tocstyle` package using `\usepackage{tocstyle}`. Then, we use the `\tableofcontents` command to generate the table of contents.

The table of contents is generated automatically based on the section headings in the document. By default, `tocstyle` uses a simple two-column format with the section titles in bold and the page numbers in smaller font.

You can customize the appearance of the table of contents by using additional options with the `\tableofcontents` command. For example, you can change the font size, add custom styles for different types of headings, or add custom page numbers.

Here's an example of how to customize the table of contents using the `tocstyle` package:
```latex
\documentclass{article}
\usepackage{tocstyle}

\setcounter{tocdepth}{3} % Set the toc depth to 3 (i.e., include sections and subsections)
\setlength{\cftsecnumwidth}{5em} % Set the width of section numbers
\setlength{\cftsubsecnumwidth}{3em} % Set the width of subsection numbers
\renewcommand{\contentsname}{Table of Contents} % Customize the name of the table of contents

\begin{document}

\tableofcontents

\section{Introduction}
This is the introduction section.

\section{Methodology}
This is the methodology section.

\section{Results}
This is the results section.

\section{Discussion}
This is the discussion section.

\end{document}
```
In this example, we set the toc depth to 3 using `\setcounter{tocdepth}{3}`, which means that the table of contents will include all sections and subsections in the document. We also set the width of section and subsection numbers using `\setlength{\cftsecnumwidth}{5em}` and `\setlength{\cftsubsecnumwidth}{3em}`, respectively. Finally, we customize the name of the table of contents using `\renewcommand{\contentsname}{Table of Contents}`.

