Here is an implementation of a binary search algorithm in Python:
```
def binary_search(arr, x):
    # Check if the array is empty
    if len(arr) == 0:
        return -1

    # Check if the element is at the beginning or end of the array
    if arr[0] == x:
        return 0
    elif arr[-1] == x:
        return len(arr) - 1

    # Find the middle index
    mid = len(arr) // 2

    # Check if the element is in the middle of the array
    if arr[mid] == x:
        return mid

    # Check if the element is greater than the middle element
    if arr[mid] < x:
        return binary_search(arr[mid:], x)

    # Check if the element is less than the middle element
    else:
        return binary_search(arr[:mid], x)
```
This algorithm works by dividing the array in half at each step and checking if the element is in the middle of the array. If the element is in the middle of the array, the algorithm returns the index of the element. If the element is greater than the middle element, the algorithm recursively searches the right half of the array. If the element is less than the middle element, the algorithm recursively searches the left half of the array. This process continues until the element is found or the array is empty.

